<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nazi-Soviet Pact, German-Soviet Non-Aggression Pact</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact was the non-aggression treaty signed by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 23 August 1939. Its secret protocols divided eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> into spheres of influence, opening the way to the joint invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and to the Soviet occupation and annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> in 1940. Germany broke the pact by invading the Soviet Union in June 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact.jpg" alt="Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Template:Helmut Laux · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H27337,_Moskau,_Stalin_und_Ribbentrop_im_Kreml.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Talks between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> broke down after the Soviet Union was excluded from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> of September 1938. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and France had judged Soviet participation in the conference dangerous and useless, partly because of Hitler's anti-Soviet rhetoric, while the Soviet leadership believed that the West wanted to encourage German aggression in the East. Rapprochement between the Soviet Union and Nazi Germany began in early 1939. In May Stalin replaced his foreign minister Maxim Litvinov, who had advocated rapprochement with the West, with Vyacheslav Molotov. Contacts at first went through economic negotiations, and in early August the two sides began to discuss a political agreement, finding common ground in their opposition to the capitalist democracies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The Treaty of Non-Aggression between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics was dated 23 August 1939 and signed by Molotov and the German foreign minister Joachim von Ribbentrop. It ran for ten years and provided for consultation, arbitration of disagreements, neutrality if either party went to war with a third power, and no membership of any group aimed at the other.</p><p>The Secret Additional Protocol provided for the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Latvia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> were allotted to the Soviet sphere, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> region, to the German one. The existence of the protocol was rumoured in the West and was proved only when it was made public at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a>. The agreement stunned governments, the press, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s allies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and foreign Communist parties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 1 September 1939. On 17 September, stating concern for ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a> there, Stalin ordered the Soviet invasion. The German–Soviet Boundary and Friendship Treaty of 28 September then drew a new border on former Polish territory and moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> into the Soviet sphere. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> on 30 November in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a>, Finland ceded parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, Kuusamo and Salla, and Hanko was leased as a naval base. About 422,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a>, 12 per cent of the population, lost their homes.</p><p>In mid-June 1940 Soviet NKVD troops raided border posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>. Their state administrations were replaced by Soviet cadres, who deported or killed 34,250 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>, 75,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> and almost 60,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>. Elections with a single pro-Soviet candidate for many positions produced assemblies that asked for admission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, which was granted. Parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and Northern Bukovina, were also annexed. The pact ended on 22 June 1941 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a>. Ribbentrop was convicted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and executed in 1946.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</b> of 1939 recorded the Soviet interest in Bessarabia, and in 1940, under pressure from Nazi Germany or the Soviet Union, Romania ceded most of the lands it had gained after 1918, keeping only the southern halves of Bukovina and Transylvania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</b> of 1939 the Soviet Union forced Estonia to accept military bases, and in June 1940 the government did not resist the Soviet occupation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</b>, the Soviet Union forced Latvia to accept military bases in October 1939, delivered an ultimatum on 16 June 1940 and occupied the country the next day.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</b> first assigned Lithuania to Germany, but after Smetona refused to join the invasion of Poland it passed to the Soviet sphere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>One week after Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union signed the <b>Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</b>, Germany and Slovakia attacked Poland on 1 September 1939, and the Soviets attacked eastern Poland on 17 September.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</b> of August 1939 the Soviet Union invaded eastern Poland, fought the costly Winter War against Finland and annexed the Baltic states and Bessarabia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Military</a></li></ul></section>
